  "query_preview": "..\n\nLongwave pattern consists of a ridge over the western half of the \nconus and troughing over the northeastern conus with the Great Lakes \ntransitioning out of the trough tonight. Dry northwesterly flow and \nsurface ridging will keep the area quiet tonight with the main \nquestion being how cold does it get with the thermal trough overhead \ndipping below 5C at 850mb? Center of the high will be to just to the \nsouth with gradient winds likely remaining in the 5 knot range \novernight. There will also be some passing high cloud, but may not \nbe enough to blanket the area to keep temps up. Afternoon min \ndewpoints have fallen into the upper 30s which shows the potential \nfor low temps tonight which still look to fall to around 40. \n\nHeights begin to rise Friday allow for a slight increase in temps, \nthough they will likely remain in the 60s again. Low impact feature \nwe continue to watch in the model space is a shortwave riding along \nthe elevated warm front that will lift through Friday night. The \nshortwave looks to activate over IA and northern IL Friday afternoon \nwith as the low level jet surges northward into it. As the wave \ndrifts east though it separates from the jet which remains west of \nLake MI so should see a weakening trend with any showers over lower \nMI, possibly no rain at all, but there is still a good theta e surge \nwhich could be enough to bring some showers along the state line \nafter 00Z Saturday. \n\nStill looking at a warm up on Saturday as southwesterly flow ramps \nup behind the warm front and the thermal ridge folds into the Great \nLakes. ECMWF has cooled off a few degrees since yesterday with 850mb \ntemps peaking around 15-16C instead of 18C that was advertised \npreviously. GFS is still in the 16-17C range. So will keep highs in \nthe upper 70s and we'll see if 80s will still be possible as we get \ncloser. \n\nLater in the weekend will be dictated by the track of the remnants \nof Hurricane Isaias which is forecast to lift northward toward the \nOhio Valley through the weekend before getting shunted off to the \neast. Still indications that some degree of the outer portions of \nthe northern precip shield will lift into southern MI before pealing \noff to the east, but now it appears the deeper plume of moisture \nwill stay to the southeast limiting potential for heavier rainfall \nlocally.",
